    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Academic-Lead-Khuda-Program_0154593-3": {
      "title": "Academic Lead Khuda Program",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPart time (1 day per week), 2-year fixed term opportunity Exciting opportunity to provide academic leadership and strategic direction for the University’s largest philanthropic donation – The Khuda Women in STEM Program. Offering a competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation along with a comprehensive range of employee benefits About the opportunity As the Academic Lead – Khuda Women in STEM Program , you will provide high-level academic leadership and act as the University’s principal ambassador for this major philanthropic initiative addressing the underrepresentation of women in STEM.  Working closely with senior University stakeholders, the program donor and external stakeholders, you will champion the program’s strategic vision, priorities and impact across the University and externally.  You will provide academic advice to ensure the program is underpinned by rigorous evidence, aligned with best practice and maintains a high standard of academic quality. With a high degree of autonomy, you will exercise independent judgement, contribute academic insight and thought leadership to University governance and support the ongoing development, delivery and evaluation of the program to ensure the program continues to deliver meaningful outcomes for women and girls in STEM.   You’ll also play a role in strengthening the program’s profile through engaging with donors and philanthropic partners in collaboration with Advancement, and representing the program through external forums, media and public events, and contribute to its evidence base and long-term impact.  Your key responsibilities will be to: provide high-level academic leadership and act as the University’s principal ambassador for the Khuda Women in STEM Program  champion the program’s strategic vision, priorities and impact across the University and externally  provide authoritative academic advice, ensuring program activities are evidence-based, aligned with best practice and underpinned by academic quality  maintain a national or international academic profile relevant to the program’s objectives, contributing to its evidence base, credibility, and influence  build and maintain relationships with donors, philanthropic partners, academic, industry and professional stakeholders for program advocacy and support long term funding outcomes.   represent the program in external forums, including media, public events, and sector engagement opportunities, to elevate its profile, credibility, and influence.  Contribute to University governance, committees and strategic initiatives, providing academic insight and thought leadership to inform decision-making  About you     hold a doctorate or PhD in a relevant field  a reputation at a national or international level, extensive academic experience and an established research profile related to the Program’s objectives.   a strong research track record and scholarly contribution  a wide network of established relationships with academic, industry and professional partners and stakeholders  demonstrated experience building partnerships and engaging with diverse stakeholders  demonstrated strategic leadership, thinking and decision-making capability with the ability to provide authoritative academic advice relevant to the Program’s objectives.   highly developed communication, presentation, influencing and stakeholder engagement skills  Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Nicole Turle, Recruitment Operations by email to nicole.turle@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 13 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Chippendale/Senior-Software-Developer-Marine-Robotic-Systems_0152973-2": {
      "title": "Senior Software Developer Marine Robotic Systems",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term 12-month position at the Australian Centre for Robotics Work with a world leading marine robotics group exploring the translation of robotics research into practice Base Salary HEO7 - 8 $117,133 - $147,858 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity The University of Sydney’s Australian Centre for Robotics (ACFR) is one of Australia’s leading robotics research groups and undertakes fundamental and applied research in the area of field robotics. Our marine systems group is focused on the development of autonomous marine vehicle systems for marine survey and has recently been awarded an Australian Economic Accelerator (AEA) Innovate grant to explore the commercialisation of our Autonomous Underwater Vehicle (AUV) technology.  One of the key objectives of this project is to demonstrate the vehicle capabilities and validate operations in support of marine survey-as-a-service operations across several industry sectors. Key project outcomes will include demonstration missions at sites around Australia and engaging with a range of key stakeholders as part of our market validation program. In collaboration with a strong existing scientific and industrial userbase, we will validate the developed tools and demonstrate how important scientific insights directly relevant to ecosystem management and commercial development in offshore industries can be derived from underwater imagery and associated data collected by AUV systems. Applications include environmental monitoring and impact assessment, climate change science, marine park management, marine archaeology, deep-sea geology, asset inspection and defence applications.  Successful candidates will be expected to spend some time in the field supporting operations and validating the outcomes of their work in real-world trials to demonstrate capability. To support this work, we are seeking a Senior Software Developer with experience in marine robotic systems.  The successful candidate will play a crucial role in developing and refining the core functionalities that enable autonomous operations, including navigation, data processing, communication, and mission planning. Their primary responsibility will be developing communication systems and user-friendly command and control interfaces, allowing operators to easily configure missions and monitor AUV performance remotely as well as data visualisation tools and cloud-based platforms for real-time mission analysis.  They will also work on software that allows the vehicles to navigate and adapt to dynamic underwater environments without human intervention, including algorithms for real-time sensor fusion, autonomous decision-making, and path optimisation to ensure the AUV can efficiently execute complex tasks such as underwater mapping or surveillance. This role is offered across HEO7 and HEO8. At HEO7, you will have demonstrated experience and work independently, contributing to software development and supporting system design. At HEO8, you will bring deeper expertise, operate with a high degree of independence, and take a lead role in shaping and refining overall software design. Your key responsibilities will be to: work closely with researchers, engineers and stakeholders to deliver software solutions aligned to research goals lead development of software components and applications for marine robotic systems design system architecture to optimise performance and reliability implement testing methodologies and resolve bugs and errors plan and execute software deployments maintain technical documentation and support compliance, risk and safety requirements About you postgraduate qualification or equivalent experience in software engineering, robotics or related discipline demonstrated experience developing software solutions in agile or scrum environments strong programming experience (e.g. Python, C++, JavaScript) experience with robotics systems including software development, integration and debugging experience designing and implementing testing frameworks and deployment pipelines experience working across embedded systems, web applications and user interface development. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Liam Newson and Nicole Feain, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 20 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Chippendale/Senior-Mechanical-and-Production-Engineer_0155029-2": {
      "title": "Senior Mechanical and Production Engineer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term 12-month position at the Australian Centre for Robotics Work with a world leading marine robotics groups exploring the translation of robotics research into practice Base Salary HEO7 - 8 $117,133 - $147,858 + 17% superannuation   About the opportunity   The University of Sydney’s Australian Centre for Robotics (ACFR) is one of Australia’s leading robotics research groups and undertakes fundamental and applied research in the area of field robotics. Our marine systems group is focused on the development of autonomous marine vehicle systems for marine survey and has recently been awarded an Australian Economic Accelerator (AEA) Innovate grant to explore the commercialisation of our Autonomous Underwater Vehicle (AUV) technology.  One of the key objectives of this project is to demonstrate the vehicle capabilities and validate operations in support of marine survey-as-a-service operations across several industry sectors.   Key project outcomes will include demonstration missions at sites around Australia and engaging with a range of key stakeholders as part of our market validation program. In collaboration with a strong existing scientific and industrial userbase, we will validate the developed tools and demonstrate how important scientific insights directly relevant to ecosystem management and commercial development in offshore industries can be derived from underwater imagery and associated data collected by AUV systems. Applications include environmental monitoring and impact assessment, climate change science, marine park management, marine archaeology, deep-sea geology, asset inspection and defence applications.  Successful candidates will be expected to spend some time in the field supporting operations and validating the outcomes of their work in real-world trials to demonstrate capability.   To support this work, we are seeking a Senior Mechanical and Production Engineer with experience in marine robotic systems.  The successful candidate will play a crucial role in developing and refining the core vehicle designs, including mechanical and electronic systems. Their primary responsibility will be developing production ready versions of the vehicle systems and supporting the manufacturing and maintenance of AUV systems.  They will also work on refining and hardening vehicle designs and supporting validation of the systems in the field.   This role is offered across HEO7 and HEO8. At HEO7, you will have demonstrated experience and work independently, contributing to mechanical and electronic design and supporting system design. At HEO8, you will bring deeper expertise, operate with a high degree of independence, and take a lead role in shaping and refining overall system design and production processes.   Your key responsibilities will be to: work closely with researchers, engineers and stakeholders to deliver vehicle design solutions aligned to research goals lead development of mechanical and electronic components and applications for marine robotic systems design vehicle systems to optimise performance and reliability implement mechanical and electronic testing methodologies and resolve bugs and errors plan and execute field deployments and validation maintain technical documentation and support compliance, risk and safety requirements About you postgraduate qualification or equivalent experience in mechanical engineering, robotics or related discipline demonstrated experience developing mechanical and electronic solutions that are production ready strong design experience (e.g. SolidWorks, Altium) experience with robotics systems including mechanical and electronic development, integration and debugging experience working across embedded systems, advanced manufacturing and production systems. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Liam Newson and Nicole Feain, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 20 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Research-Fellow_0153314-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, 2 year fixed term position. Located on the Camperdown Campus, University of Sydney Opportunity to contribute to the recently established Centre for Sustainability, AI and Life Sciences (SAILS) at the Faculty of Engineering Base Salary Academic Level A $117,936 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The Centre for Sustainability, AI and Life Sciences (SAILS) brings together AI researchers and domain experts from across the University of Sydney, covering a broad spectrum of areas from molecular biology and health to chemistry and clean energy. We are looking for Research Fellows to join the Centre to work closely with academics across disciplines on research projects within SAILS. In addition, you will be given the opportunity to publish and present your work at leading AI conferences and in the major journals of your application area, and to mentor HDR and Honours students. Applications are welcome across the full breadth of AI for sustainability and the life sciences. We are particularly interested in: machine learning for molecular and omics data, including representation learning for biological sequences and structures, and the integration of multiple omics layers machine learning combined with first principles and multiscale simulation for the design of molecules, catalysts and materials, with applications in clean energy and decarbonisation AI for clinical and health research using heterogeneous data (free text, structured records, images, audio), including locally hosted and fine-tuned large language models computer vision for biological and medical imaging, including three-dimensional, spatial and temporal image data trustworthy AI for sensitive and distributed data, including privacy-preserving and federated learning, uncertainty quantification, and efficient real-time inference in resource-constrained settings Your key responsibilities will be to: conduct high-quality research independently and collaboratively to advance the state-of-the-art in AI and its application to sustainability and the life sciences publish research outcomes in leading international conferences and journals collaborate with researchers across disciplines adhere to and promote the University's health, safety and ethical guidelines. About you Successful applicants will have: a PhD (or near completion) in computer science, statistics, engineering, or a physical, environmental or life science discipline demonstrated expertise in AI and machine learning, including experience building and evaluating models in frameworks such as PyTorch, and running workflows in HPC environments experience applying machine learning to scientific problems, ideally in a sustainability or life sciences domain proven capability for independent research, evidenced by high-quality publications excellent interpersonal and communication skills, including the ability to work with collaborators across disciplines commitment to fostering an inclusive and respectful workplace, and an understanding of and adherence to University policies and procedures related to health and safety. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, research statement and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Liam Newson and Nicole Feain, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 28 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Upskilling-Project-Officer--Targeted-_0153157-1": {
      "title": "Upskilling Project Officer (Targeted)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPart-time (28 hours per week), 12-month fixed-term opportunity, based on Camperdown Campus on lands of the Gadigal people of the Eora Nation Support the delivery of a nationally significant mental health initiative and help strengthen eating disorder identification and early intervention across Australia Base Salary $107,312 (pro-rata) + 17% superannuation About the opportunity This is a Targeted Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Position. While we welcome applications from all candidates, this is a targeted role for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. As part of our commitment to diversity and inclusion, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates will be assessed as a priority. This Exemption is claimed under Section 21 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977. This is an exciting opportunity for a passionate, highly-organised and experienced project coordinator to contribute to an innovative national eating disorder initiative that upskills clinicians and embeds screening and identification within the workforce in headspace, Medicare Mental Health and Primary Health Networks nationally. The position will develop strong working relationships with key organisations in the mental health sector, and contribute to the delivery of a highly successful national youth mental health system change initiative. The successful candidate will become part of an established project team that is embedding eating disorder training, identification, and early intervention within frontline mental health services across Australia – the first project of its kind. In addition to general project coordination activities, the position will support engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander stakeholders and help to ensure all project activities are inclusive, respectful and responsive to the needs and strengths of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities. What You Will Do As a Project Officer, you will provide operational project management support. Working closely with the Project Manager, you will coordinate project activities, maintain project documentation and reporting, support stakeholder engagement, and help ensure project deliverables are achieved on time and to a high standard. Your key responsibilities will be to: Coordinate project activities, work plans, and timelines to support the successful delivery of project initiatives and ensure project objectives are achieved. Monitor project progress, deliverables, and milestones, identifying risks or issues and implementing appropriate actions to maintain project momentum. Prepare, maintain, and coordinate project documentation, resources, data, and reporting to support effective project governance and decision-making. Facilitate project logistics, including scheduling meetings, training activities, workshops, and events, while coordinating stakeholders and venue arrangements. Support the integration of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander perspectives and cultural considerations into project resources, workforce development activities and stakeholder engagement strategies. Build and maintain productive relationships with internal and external stakeholders to support collaboration, communication, and successful project outcomes. Support the development, implementation, and continuous improvement of project methodologies, training platforms, and operational processes. Why work with us The InsideOut Institute is Australia's national institute for research, clinical excellence, and policy innovation in eating disorders, and contributes to initiatives that strengthen the capability of mental health services to identify and respond to eating disorders across the country.We are committed to creating a culturally safe, supportive and inclusive workplace for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. Key benefits include: generous cultural leave and flexible working arrangements access to Indigenous professional and career development programs, including mentoring, leadership programs, and development funding participation in an internal Indigenous staff network, providing cultural connection, peer support and professional development opportunities YURA network tax-efficient salary packaging options for cars, laptops, and additional contributions to your superannuation fund About you Bachelor’s degree in a relevant field (e.g. psychology, nursing, social work, public health). Qualification in Project Management/coordination and/or Experience in health-related project coordination/management. Experience working within health/mental services (e.g. headspace, primary care) and/or supporting the implementation of new programs within healthcare or related environments. Demonstrated ability to support culturally responsive project delivery through respectful engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander stakeholders and an understanding of the importance of incorporating Indigenous perspectives and knowledges into health and mental health initiatives. Demonstrated experience coordinating health/mental health projects and engaging effectively with diverse internal and external stakeholders to achieve shared objectives. Strong organisational and time management skills, with the ability to prioritise competing tasks, monitor progress, and meet deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to build productive relationships and collaborate effectively across multidisciplinary teams. High level of attention to detail, numeracy skills, and proficiency in the Microsoft Office Suite to support project administration, reporting, and data management. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. This is a targeted opportunity for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Roshik Prasad, Recruitment Operations by email to roshik.prasad@sydney.edu.au © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 13 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Newtown/Senior-Lecturer---Human-Genetics-and-Bioinformatics_0155956-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer - Human Genetics and Bioinformatics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, continuing position  Exciting opportunity to a Senior Lecturer within the School of Medical Sciences at the University of Sydney  Base Salary starting at $162,347 - $187,198 + 17% superannuation  About the opportunity The School of Medical Sciences is recognised as one of the leading centres for medical science education and research. With over 100 years of excellence, the school is committed to advancing knowledge, training future leaders, and delivering impactful research across a range of disciplines. The School of Medical Sciences is seeking a Senior Lecturer in Human Genetics and/or Bioinformatics who will make significant contributions to research, education and academic activities while building a strong reputation as an authoritative figure within their discipline. The role will contribute to high-quality teaching and learning experiences, curriculum development, research leadership, supervision of higher degree research students and the establishment of collaborations that enhance the university’s research profile and reputation. Your key responsibilities will be to: build and maintain a reputation for academic excellence through innovative and impactful research, scholarship and education that advances knowledge and practice within the school and discipline secure, lead and contribute to competitive research funding and grant opportunities, and develop collaborations and partnerships that enhance the research profile, impact and reputation of the University deliver and coordinate high-quality teaching and learning experiences, including the supervision and successful completion of higher degree research students develop, review and enhance curricula, programs and learning resources to achieve excellent educational outcomes and an outstanding student experience provide academic leadership in teaching, curriculum innovation, research training and supervision within the School and Faculty mentor, support and develop academic staff, fostering excellence in teaching, research and professional practice establish external partnerships and engage in translational research that delivers impactful outcomes in research, commercialisation, policy and practice engage in professional, community, alumni and public activities that promote the discipline and strengthen the University's reputation and impact contribute to a positive and inclusive workplace culture that values collaboration, diversity, excellence and achievement support and advance the strategic priorities of the University, Faculty and School through leadership, service and engagement undertake other duties appropriate to the classification level as required. About you doctorate (PhD) and relevant professional qualifications in a discipline aligned with the position sustained record of high-quality, original research, scholarly achievement and research impact within their discipline demonstrated success in securing, leading and contributing to competitive research funding, grants and research collaborations strong national reputation and an emerging international profile in their area of research expertise strong record of excellence in teaching and learning across a range of educational settings, with experience in educational design, delivery and evaluation demonstrated expertise in curriculum development and a commitment to pedagogical innovation and continuous improvement experience supervising higher degree research students and mentoring academic staff to support their development and success an established network of academic, industry, government and professional partners, with the ability to build productive collaborations and partnerships highly developed skills in research leadership, critical thinking, strategic planning, project management and informed decision-making excellent communication, interpersonal and presentation skills, with the ability to engage effectively with diverse stakeholders and work collaboratively across disciplines and sectors demonstrated commitment to fostering an inclusive, collegial and high-performing academic environment. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community which reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.   For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Ajaypal Singh, Recruitment Operations by email to ajaypal.singh@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 13 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Hospital-Manager_0156159-1": {
      "title": "Hospital Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nYour chance to have a marked impact on the future of the veterinary profession. Full time, Fixed Term Parental Leave Cover until 13th February 2028 Located onsite on Camperdown Campus Exciting opportunity to contribute to the development and implementation of hospital strategy in a competitive veterinary market Base Salary HEO 10 (38 hours) $176,461 p.a. + 17% superannuation About the opportunity This is an exciting opportunity to join the University of Sydney Veterinary Teaching Hospital. As part of the School of Veterinary Science which is ranked #1 in Australia, the University Veterinary Teaching Hospital Sydney (UVTHS) provides a clinical learning experience for veterinary students with staff engaged in professional training programs through provision of veterinary services to the public. We are currently seeking a Hospital Manager to play a critical role in the operational management of the hospital for the period of parental leave cover and to contribute to the development and implementation of hospital strategy. Together with the Director of Vet Operations, the role oversees the execution of services which provide clinical teaching, research and business activities in a competitive veterinary market. UVTHS is a combined referral and primary care small animal hospital with access to a range of specialist services, expertise, and equipment to offer excellent care to our patients. We benefit from a loyal clientele who always want the very best for their pets. This is your chance to have a marked impact on the future of the veterinary profession through your contribution and support of the clinical service and training of students.  The key benefits be stimulated by an academic environment focused on teaching and research opportunity for you to learn and enhance your skills and experience in a unique teaching and research environment of a veterinary teaching hospital salary packaging available access to employee assistance programs (EAP) at no cost access to fitness passport, as well as discounted rates on university sports and fitness centres located on the Camperdown campus, 4 km from the city centre, in a convenient central location with ample public transport options. Discounted Staff Parking is also available on site. In the position you will be providing operational leadership and contributing to the development of the hospital's strategy   managing the development of business plans for the delivery of services for the hospital and ensuring the effective implementation of new initiatives the critical point of contact with oversight of WH&S and operational requirements across the span of each week for the Hospital overseeing the continuous improvement of processes to support efficient and effective hospital operations responsible for the functional leadership and management of professional support staff in hospital. Manage the responsibilities of their workload, safety, mentoring, training, performance reviews and staff development managing building and space allocation and all local infrastructure upgrade and redevelopment and equipment projects managing risks and workplace health and safety for the hospital that might impact staff welfare and the efficient and effective operation of the Hospital services and the teaching program working with senior leaders to manage the efficient utilisation and deployment of resources for the hospital in line with the broader School strategy contributing to the development and design of the hospital's external engagement strategy. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement  At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Masaoka, Recruitment Operations by email to recruitment.sea@sydney.edu.au. © The University of Sydney Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 13 September 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
